1
00:00:23,816 --> 00:00:26,318
(ELECTRICITY CRACKLING)

2
00:00:26,402 --> 00:00:29,029
BOY: You are never more alive
than when you're a teenager.

3
00:00:29,113 --> 00:00:30,322
(ELECTRICITY ZAPPING)

4
00:00:30,406 --> 00:00:32,241
Your brain is flush
with chemicals

5
00:00:32,324 --> 00:00:36,620
that can turn your life into
a story of epic proportions.

6
00:00:36,704 --> 00:00:39,165
An A-minus feels like
the Pulitzer,

7
00:00:39,248 --> 00:00:42,877
a lonely Saturday night
is an eternity of solitude,

8
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:47,047
and your lab partner becomes
the great love of your life.

9
00:00:47,131 --> 00:00:49,759
And yet, by the start
of my senior year,

10
00:00:49,842 --> 00:00:52,219
nothing interesting
or remarkable

11
00:00:52,303 --> 00:00:54,096
had ever happened to me.

12
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:57,308
- I was grateful for my life.
- (LAUGHS)

13
00:00:57,391 --> 00:00:59,935
I had my best friends...
All two of them...

14
00:01:00,019 --> 00:01:01,020
Relax.
You got this.

15
00:01:01,103 --> 00:01:02,354
...parents who met

16
00:01:02,438 --> 00:01:04,565
and fell in love
when they were my age,

17
00:01:04,648 --> 00:01:06,525
and my writing.

18
00:01:06,609 --> 00:01:09,153
(READING)

19
00:01:17,953 --> 00:01:19,371
But a platform was pointless

20
00:01:19,455 --> 00:01:21,957
unless I actually had
something to say.

21
00:01:22,041 --> 00:01:23,709
And I didn't,

22
00:01:23,793 --> 00:01:26,921
because nothing worth writing
about had ever happened to me.

23
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:29,256
(CLOSES LAPTOP)

24
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,635
Then, on the day
I found out about the job,

25
00:01:32,718 --> 00:01:34,220
something finally happened.

26
00:01:40,309 --> 00:01:42,311
(CHATTERING)

27
00:01:47,358 --> 00:01:48,859
(SIGHS)

28
00:02:05,543 --> 00:02:07,086
MAN: Hi, guys.
Thanks for swinging by.

29
00:02:07,169 --> 00:02:09,505
Come on in.
You want us both?

30
00:02:09,588 --> 00:02:10,965
Yes, both of you would be great.

31
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:12,466
Come on.

32
00:02:14,426 --> 00:02:16,595
MAN: Grace just transferred
here from East River,

33
00:02:16,679 --> 00:02:19,348
where she spent two years
writing for the Gazette.

34
00:02:19,431 --> 00:02:22,726
You both possess remarkable
depth for writers your age.

35
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:27,064
Which is why I think
that you two will make

36
00:02:27,147 --> 00:02:30,150
a truly dynamic
editor-in-chief duo.

37
00:02:31,026 --> 00:02:32,069
No.
What?

38
00:02:32,862 --> 00:02:34,655
Uh, one more time?

39
00:02:34,738 --> 00:02:37,199
I don't want to be an editor.

40
00:02:37,283 --> 00:02:38,492
But thank you.

41
00:02:40,536 --> 00:02:41,537
Um...

42
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:43,622
Hey.

43
00:02:43,706 --> 00:02:45,875
- Why'd you say no?
- What?

44
00:02:45,958 --> 00:02:48,127
Why'd you say no?

45
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,629
Why do you care?
What?

46
00:02:50,713 --> 00:02:51,922
Why do you care?

47
00:02:52,006 --> 00:02:54,300
Uh... I don't.

48
00:02:54,383 --> 00:02:57,553
It's just a good opportunity for a writer,
I guess.

49
00:02:57,636 --> 00:02:59,471
I just don't get
why you would say no.

50
00:02:59,555 --> 00:03:02,182
All right, well,
don't let it keep you up at night.

51
00:03:08,898 --> 00:03:11,400
(SIGHS)
Oh, shit.

52
00:03:11,483 --> 00:03:12,818
What?

53
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,447
I just missed my bus.

54
00:03:17,865 --> 00:03:19,700
Can't walk?

55
00:03:19,783 --> 00:03:22,036
Um... no,
I mean, I can.

56
00:03:22,119 --> 00:03:23,454
It's just far.

57
00:03:23,537 --> 00:03:26,832
Uh, that's why I take the bus.

58
00:03:40,179 --> 00:03:41,931
My house is up there.

59
00:03:43,265 --> 00:03:46,268
I have a car. I can give
you a ride if you want.

60
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:49,021
Yeah?

61
00:03:50,522 --> 00:03:52,066
Thanks.

62
00:03:57,821 --> 00:03:59,990
Hey, so you like "Nehradah"?

63
00:04:01,951 --> 00:04:04,036
Neruda.

64
00:04:04,119 --> 00:04:06,538
Oh. (LAUGHS)

65
00:04:06,622 --> 00:04:08,832
Oh. Um...

66
00:04:08,916 --> 00:04:10,584
Yeah, well,
I only read a stanza,

67
00:04:10,668 --> 00:04:13,629
but I thought
it was really beautiful.

68
00:04:14,797 --> 00:04:16,382
You think so?

69
00:04:18,759 --> 00:04:20,511
Yeah. Do you not?

70
00:04:21,929 --> 00:04:23,597
No, I just think
that's what people say

71
00:04:23,681 --> 00:04:26,767
when they read a poem
that they don't understand.

72
00:04:30,938 --> 00:04:32,439
Hmm.

73
00:04:32,523 --> 00:04:34,066
(SNIFFS)

74
00:04:39,363 --> 00:04:40,781
GRACE: Finish it.

75
00:04:42,366 --> 00:04:44,660
And let me know if you
still feel the same way.

76
00:04:48,706 --> 00:04:50,207
(BICYCLE BELL DINGS)

77
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:52,584
(TRAIN HORN BLOWING IN DISTANCE)

78
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:54,837
Huh.
It's a cool place.

79
00:04:56,171 --> 00:04:58,799
How come you walk to
school if you have a car?

80
00:04:58,882 --> 00:05:00,300
I don't like driving.

81
00:05:00,384 --> 00:05:02,594
- Do you have a license?
- Yeah.

82
00:05:02,678 --> 00:05:06,181
("TAKE CARE" BY BEACH HOUSE
PLAYING OVER SPEAKERS)

83
00:05:06,265 --> 00:05:10,352
<i>♪ I'd take care of you ♪</i>

84
00:05:10,436 --> 00:05:12,146
It's a good song.

85
00:05:12,229 --> 00:05:17,067
<i>♪ If you ask me to ♪</i>

86
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:21,947
(SONG CONTINUES FAINTLY)

87
00:05:24,658 --> 00:05:25,868
(ENGINE AND MUSIC STOP)

88
00:05:25,951 --> 00:05:28,203
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

89
00:05:37,629 --> 00:05:40,340
You live in a house like this,
but you don't have a car?

90
00:05:40,424 --> 00:05:43,451
I guess my grades aren't
good enough for a car, so...

91
00:05:43,466 --> 00:05:44,470
Hmm.

92
00:05:47,264 --> 00:05:48,432
Thanks a lot.

93
00:05:56,356 --> 00:05:57,775
Uh...

94
00:05:58,942 --> 00:06:00,319
Well, I'll see you tomorrow?

95
00:06:02,571 --> 00:06:03,739
Okay.

96
00:06:07,284 --> 00:06:10,287
Henry.
What's up, Suds?

97
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:12,122
You, uh, just get back
or something?

98
00:06:12,206 --> 00:06:13,749
SUDS: Mmm, no.
Just leaving.

99
00:06:13,832 --> 00:06:15,334
I'm late.

100
00:06:15,417 --> 00:06:17,044
So, uh,
who's the girl?

101
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:19,588
- What girl?
- SUDS: The one that drove Henry home.

102
00:06:19,671 --> 00:06:22,216
A girl drove you
home? (CHUCKLES)

103
00:06:22,299 --> 00:06:24,718
SUDS: She left her car
here and walked away.

104
00:06:24,802 --> 00:06:25,803
(WHISPERS)
Run.

105
00:06:25,886 --> 00:06:27,721
Yeah, when are you gonna
move back out again?

106
00:06:27,805 --> 00:06:30,641
Ignore her.
She's declared war on love.

107
00:06:30,724 --> 00:06:33,143
No, just on cheating assholes.

108
00:06:33,227 --> 00:06:35,062
Will you see him today? Oh,
my God.

109
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:36,271
He's my attending, Mom.

110
00:06:36,355 --> 00:06:37,981
I'll see him
several times today.

111
00:06:38,065 --> 00:06:40,818
So, how was the first day back?

112
00:06:40,901 --> 00:06:42,986
Good. Yeah.
It was good.

113
00:06:43,070 --> 00:06:44,738
Did you get it?

114
00:06:44,822 --> 00:06:46,532
HENRY: Uh...

115
00:06:46,615 --> 00:06:47,866
- Yeah.
- MOTHER: Oh.

116
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:49,785
SUDS: Yeah, you did.
(CHUCKLES)

117
00:06:49,868 --> 00:06:50,869
Congratulations, sweetie.

118
00:06:50,953 --> 00:06:52,788
Nice work.

119
00:06:52,871 --> 00:06:55,457
SUDS: Okay,
got to go. Love you, guys.

120
00:06:55,541 --> 00:06:57,751
So, who's the girl?

121
00:06:57,835 --> 00:07:00,129
("TAKE CARE" BY BEACH HOUSE
PLAYING OVER SPEAKERS)

122
00:07:02,381 --> 00:07:06,802
<i>♪ I'd take care of you ♪</i>

123
00:07:08,846 --> 00:07:13,142
<i>♪ If you'd ask me to ♪</i>

124
00:07:15,018 --> 00:07:21,692
<i>♪ In a year or two ♪</i>

125
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:24,695
<i>♪ Oh-oh-oh ♪</i>

126
00:07:31,702 --> 00:07:34,121
HENRY: "I do not love you
as if you were the salt-rose,

127
00:07:34,204 --> 00:07:38,167
topaz or arrow of carnations
that propagate fire.

128
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:40,711
I love you as certain
dark things are to be loved,

129
00:07:40,794 --> 00:07:43,172
in secret, between the shadow
and the soul."

130
00:07:43,255 --> 00:07:45,174
"...the shadow and the soul."

131
00:07:45,257 --> 00:07:47,301
"I love you like the plant
that does not bloom,

132
00:07:47,384 --> 00:07:49,386
but carries
within itself, hidden,

133
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:51,096
the light of those flowers."

134
00:07:51,180 --> 00:07:54,141
"...thanks to your love,
lives darkly in my body..."

135
00:07:54,224 --> 00:07:56,894
"...a certain solid fragrance
that has risen from the earth."

136
00:07:56,977 --> 00:08:00,564
I love you without knowing
how or when or from where.

137
00:08:00,647 --> 00:08:03,692
I love you straightforwardly,
without complexities or pride,

138
00:08:03,775 --> 00:08:05,402
in which there is no you nor I,

139
00:08:05,485 --> 00:08:09,239
so close that your hand
upon my chest is mine.

140
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:12,826
So close that your hand upon
my chest is mine.

141
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:14,828
(SIGHS)

142
00:08:16,288 --> 00:08:19,208
"So close that your eyes close
as I dream."

143
00:08:20,709 --> 00:08:23,454
I have no idea what you said,
but it worked.

144
00:08:23,469 --> 00:08:24,379
What?

145
00:08:24,463 --> 00:08:27,424
I think the newspaper's gonna
be a really good thing for her.

146
00:08:28,967 --> 00:08:33,555
We will have four issues... the
first in November, the last in June.

147
00:08:33,639 --> 00:08:36,516
The first three are
garden-variety periodicals:

148
00:08:36,600 --> 00:08:40,020
Hard-hitting news, sports,
features, the like.

149
00:08:40,103 --> 00:08:42,773
But the last one,
that's where we get creative.

150
00:08:42,856 --> 00:08:44,983
With the help of the group,
Henry will choose a theme,

151
00:08:45,067 --> 00:08:47,653
and you will have carte
blanche to explore that theme

152
00:08:47,736 --> 00:08:49,279
as you see fit.

153
00:08:49,363 --> 00:08:52,282
That last issue will be
this team's legacy,

154
00:08:52,366 --> 00:08:54,910
an opportunity to share
your accrued wisdom

155
00:08:54,993 --> 00:08:57,371
with the students behind you.

156
00:08:57,454 --> 00:08:59,998
I'm not just talking about the
four years that you've spent

157
00:09:00,082 --> 00:09:01,750
within the walls
of this building.

158
00:09:01,833 --> 00:09:04,461
I am talking bigger than that.

159
00:09:04,544 --> 00:09:10,008
I am talking about the totality
of your 17 years on this planet.

160
00:09:10,092 --> 00:09:12,469
Last but not least,
Grace here joins us

161
00:09:12,552 --> 00:09:15,138
from our neighboring school,
East River Preparatory,

162
00:09:15,222 --> 00:09:19,434
and will lend Henry a helping
hand with all things editorial.

163
00:09:19,518 --> 00:09:23,438
Now, here's what Kyle and his
team did for last year's final issue.

164
00:09:23,522 --> 00:09:26,525
Um, I'm sorry.
Uh, Kyle sucks.

165
00:09:26,608 --> 00:09:28,902
(LAUGHTER)
TEACHER: Very constructive, La.

166
00:09:28,986 --> 00:09:31,530
Well, okay, um...

167
00:09:31,613 --> 00:09:34,616
the final issue last year
did kind of suck.

168
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:36,952
(QUIET LAUGHTER)

169
00:09:38,036 --> 00:09:39,871
(SIGHS)
All right.

170
00:09:39,955 --> 00:09:41,748
(STUDENTS CHATTERING QUIETLY)

171
00:09:44,918 --> 00:09:46,920
Um, hey,
so I don't know what your thoughts...

172
00:09:47,004 --> 00:09:48,797
So, I'm not writing.

173
00:09:50,257 --> 00:09:53,135
If you have something to say,
you can just say it yourself,

174
00:09:53,218 --> 00:09:56,388
and I'll help with, like,
editing and design and...

175
00:09:56,471 --> 00:09:58,015
I handle design.

176
00:09:59,641 --> 00:10:01,351
Well, everything
other than design then.

177
00:10:01,435 --> 00:10:04,980
But just not...
just not writing.

178
00:10:05,063 --> 00:10:06,940
And I'm assistant editor,

179
00:10:07,024 --> 00:10:08,927
because apparently you
worked very hard for this,

180
00:10:08,942 --> 00:10:11,069
so it should be all yours.

181
00:10:12,738 --> 00:10:14,656
HENRY: Looks like
Cora's into you again.

182
00:10:14,740 --> 00:10:16,033
Yo, what is the deal with her?

183
00:10:16,116 --> 00:10:18,618
She's making eyes at me,
laughing at all my jokes,

184
00:10:18,702 --> 00:10:21,496
and it's like this
air pocket that's, like,

185
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:24,708
connecting us, and it's just filled with,
like, marshmallows,

186
00:10:24,791 --> 00:10:28,920
gummy bears, and freakin'
bubble gum and sparklies,

187
00:10:29,004 --> 00:10:30,589
and it's just like,
if I eat it,

188
00:10:30,672 --> 00:10:33,842
I'm gonna get another cavity
like last year, and I can't.

189
00:10:33,925 --> 00:10:35,052
Hmm,
good metaphor.

190
00:10:35,135 --> 00:10:37,054
Thank you.
I appreciate it.

191
00:10:37,137 --> 00:10:38,388
Hey, Grace.
Hankard.

192
00:10:38,472 --> 00:10:39,848
I, um...
This is our bus!

193
00:10:39,931 --> 00:10:41,651
...actually didn't get
a chance to tell you,

194
00:10:41,725 --> 00:10:43,393
I finished that poem.

195
00:10:43,477 --> 00:10:46,146
And, um... yeah.

196
00:10:46,229 --> 00:10:47,647
I still think it's beautiful,

197
00:10:47,731 --> 00:10:50,692
but not in the, uh,
normal kind of bullshit way.

198
00:10:50,776 --> 00:10:53,111
Um, which, uh,
which I think is also

199
00:10:53,195 --> 00:10:55,238
kind of what the poem's about,
'cause, you know,

200
00:10:55,322 --> 00:11:00,077
Neruda's love for her doesn't come from,
like, uh... (SIGHS)

201
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:03,163
Sunshine and roses
and cliché stuff.

202
00:11:03,246 --> 00:11:06,875
You know, it comes from,
um, her... flaws

203
00:11:06,958 --> 00:11:09,878
and her imperfections,
like, darker places.

204
00:11:09,961 --> 00:11:11,630
LA: Hankard!
Uh, comes from...

205
00:11:11,713 --> 00:11:13,507
BOY: Come on,
you flirt!

206
00:11:13,590 --> 00:11:15,425
...I guess who she has
the potential to be

207
00:11:15,509 --> 00:11:18,387
and not just who she is.

208
00:11:20,847 --> 00:11:22,557
So, I don't know.

209
00:11:22,641 --> 00:11:25,060
So, beautiful in that kind of way,
you know.

210
00:11:27,437 --> 00:11:29,398
You're gonna
miss your bus again.

211
00:11:31,274 --> 00:11:33,110
(SINGSONGY)
I see you.

212
00:11:33,193 --> 00:11:34,903
(ROCK SONG PLAYING)
What's he doing?

213
00:11:34,986 --> 00:11:37,406
(SONG FADES)

214
00:11:37,489 --> 00:11:40,367
Um, you think I could
get another ride?

215
00:11:42,369 --> 00:11:44,079
Sure.

216
00:11:44,162 --> 00:11:48,500
<i>♪ I'd take care of you ♪</i>

217
00:11:50,585 --> 00:11:55,090
<i>♪ If you'd ask me to ♪</i>

218
00:11:57,134 --> 00:11:58,510
(CAR DOOR CLOSES)

219
00:11:58,593 --> 00:12:01,221
Thanks for letting me
drive myself home again.

220
00:12:08,645 --> 00:12:11,440
HENRY: Where do you go to?
Why do you go?

221
00:12:11,523 --> 00:12:15,360
What are your colors?
What are your moods?

222
00:12:15,444 --> 00:12:19,281
To see you, I turn.
To know you, I burn.

223
00:12:19,364 --> 00:12:21,199
(BRAKES SQUEAK OUTSIDE)

224
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:37,466
(CAR DOOR CLOSES, ENGINE STARTS)

225
00:12:58,778 --> 00:13:00,113
(SIGHS HEAVILY)

226
00:13:00,197 --> 00:13:01,990
(CORA SPEAKING SPANISH)

227
00:13:03,366 --> 00:13:05,911
- Okay. Bye.
- (LA WHISPERING) Oh, my God,

228
00:13:05,994 --> 00:13:07,621
it kills me
when she speaks Spanish.

229
00:13:07,704 --> 00:13:10,317
Don't you want to say, "What's up?"
Why? So she can ghost me again?

230
00:13:10,332 --> 00:13:12,000
I mean...
See you, guys.

231
00:13:12,083 --> 00:13:13,543
Uh, see you.

232
00:13:14,711 --> 00:13:15,754
Bye.
Uh-huh.

233
00:13:15,837 --> 00:13:17,005
CORA: Bye.

234
00:13:18,548 --> 00:13:20,258
(SIGHS) Fuck.

235
00:13:20,342 --> 00:13:21,384
Meh.

236
00:13:21,468 --> 00:13:24,638
Meh.
Meh-meh.

237
00:13:26,515 --> 00:13:28,767
(MUSIC PLAYING FAINTLY
OVER EARBUDS)

238
00:13:32,812 --> 00:13:34,231
(SIGHS)

239
00:13:36,399 --> 00:13:40,153
What was that little
"meh-meh" thing?

240
00:13:40,237 --> 00:13:43,657
Uh... oh,
um... um...

241
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:44,866
(LAUGHS)

242
00:13:44,950 --> 00:13:46,576
I just... we...
when we're just,

243
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:48,537
um, I guess, annoyed,
we just do that.

244
00:13:48,620 --> 00:13:50,205
I don't know.
Mmm.

245
00:13:50,288 --> 00:13:51,748
Um...

246
00:13:53,458 --> 00:13:55,794
Uh, you think you'd give me a ride today,
maybe?

247
00:14:02,217 --> 00:14:05,053
Hey, do you want to come in
and hang out or something?

248
00:14:07,597 --> 00:14:09,766
I can't today.

249
00:14:09,849 --> 00:14:11,017
Okay.

250
00:14:11,101 --> 00:14:12,644
Um...

251
00:14:12,727 --> 00:14:14,854
I'll just see you tomorrow then,
so... Okay.

252
00:14:22,028 --> 00:14:24,155
Henry?

253
00:14:24,239 --> 00:14:25,865
Hmm?

254
00:14:26,950 --> 00:14:29,369
Meet me here at 6:00.

255
00:14:29,452 --> 00:14:30,704
Okay.

256
00:14:30,787 --> 00:14:33,039
Yeah,
I can meet... I'll meet you there at 6:00.

257
00:14:33,123 --> 00:14:34,583
Yeah.

258
00:14:34,666 --> 00:14:36,459
Bring a loaf of bread.

259
00:14:40,714 --> 00:14:42,424
(BRAKES SQUEAK OUTSIDE)

260
00:14:42,507 --> 00:14:44,718
(VEHICLE DOOR OPENS, CLOSES)

261
00:14:46,761 --> 00:14:47,762
(CAR DOOR OPENS)

262
00:14:51,933 --> 00:14:54,686
- HENRY: What happened to your car?
- GRACE: It's back at my house.

263
00:14:54,769 --> 00:14:57,230
Did you drive it back
and then you walked here?

264
00:14:57,314 --> 00:14:58,815
We don't need it.

265
00:15:01,109 --> 00:15:03,194
- (INSECTS TRILLING)
- (BIRDS CHIRPING)

266
00:15:51,826 --> 00:15:54,037
(WATER DRIPPING)

267
00:16:02,337 --> 00:16:03,338
Come on.

268
00:16:03,421 --> 00:16:05,715
(CANE THUMPING STEADILY)

269
00:16:08,635 --> 00:16:11,054
It was a cotton mill
in the 1800s,

270
00:16:11,137 --> 00:16:13,264
and then...

271
00:16:13,348 --> 00:16:18,186
they made plastic bottles until
it shut down in 1989, I think.

272
00:16:34,411 --> 00:16:37,163
(CANE THUMPING STEADILY)

273
00:16:37,247 --> 00:16:38,331
You have to be quiet.

274
00:17:16,411 --> 00:17:18,913
- HENRY: Whoa.
- Shh.

275
00:17:18,997 --> 00:17:21,124
You'll scare them away.

276
00:17:25,336 --> 00:17:26,546
(WHISPERS)
Come here.

277
00:17:29,549 --> 00:17:31,634
(WATER SLOSHING)

278
00:17:32,677 --> 00:17:34,179
(SIGHS)

279
00:17:53,698 --> 00:17:55,742
(BOTH CHUCKLE)

280
00:18:01,539 --> 00:18:03,249
(CHUCKLES)

281
00:18:14,844 --> 00:18:17,263
Have you ever had a girlfriend?

282
00:18:17,347 --> 00:18:19,307
(CHUCKLES) Uh, no.

283
00:18:19,390 --> 00:18:21,267
Uh, not really.

284
00:18:22,018 --> 00:18:23,269
Why not?

285
00:18:24,437 --> 00:18:26,689
Uh... You ever had
a boyfriend?

286
00:18:27,732 --> 00:18:28,983
(CHUCKLES)
No.

287
00:18:30,443 --> 00:18:31,694
So what's the deal?

288
00:18:31,778 --> 00:18:33,530
I don't know.

289
00:18:33,613 --> 00:18:35,323
(LAUGHS)

290
00:18:35,406 --> 00:18:36,699
Um...

291
00:18:38,159 --> 00:18:39,786
I'm not really good
at this stuff.

292
00:18:39,869 --> 00:18:42,872
Well, I thought
you were a writer.

293
00:18:42,956 --> 00:18:45,458
Uh... yeah,
I mean, exactly.

294
00:18:45,542 --> 00:18:47,252
I could probably
go home right now

295
00:18:47,335 --> 00:18:50,630
and write you something about
why I've never had a girlfriend,

296
00:18:50,713 --> 00:18:52,674
like an essay or whatever.

297
00:18:52,757 --> 00:18:55,969
Oh, so you just
have to edit everything.

298
00:18:56,052 --> 00:18:57,262
No, it's not editing.

299
00:18:57,345 --> 00:19:01,224
It's just, writing, I think,
sometimes helps me...

300
00:19:01,307 --> 00:19:04,978
understand myself,
I guess, 'cause...

301
00:19:05,061 --> 00:19:07,501
a lot of times, I don't really
know how I feel about something

302
00:19:07,564 --> 00:19:09,399
until I really sit down
and think about it.

303
00:19:09,482 --> 00:19:13,319
But, I mean,
you haven't shared much either.

304
00:19:14,988 --> 00:19:16,906
(SIGHS)

305
00:19:16,990 --> 00:19:18,491
(CANE THUMPS)

306
00:19:18,575 --> 00:19:21,202
(WATER SLOSHES)

307
00:19:21,286 --> 00:19:22,787
(SIGHS)

308
00:19:24,539 --> 00:19:27,375
I did some of my
physical therapy in a pool.

309
00:19:29,043 --> 00:19:30,503
Nine months ago,

310
00:19:30,587 --> 00:19:32,922
the car swerved
coming around a bend

311
00:19:33,006 --> 00:19:36,634
and hit the rock barrier
and the car flipped.

312
00:19:36,718 --> 00:19:39,971
Not that it really even matters.

313
00:19:49,606 --> 00:19:51,357
When I look at that,

314
00:19:51,441 --> 00:19:54,193
it reminds me

315
00:19:54,277 --> 00:19:58,907
that people are just
the ashes of dead stars.

316
00:20:00,575 --> 00:20:03,953
We're just a collection
of atoms that...

317
00:20:04,037 --> 00:20:06,581
come together for a
brief period of time,

318
00:20:06,664 --> 00:20:10,251
and then...
we fall apart.

319
00:20:13,463 --> 00:20:16,507
When all of this is over,

320
00:20:16,591 --> 00:20:20,929
and we're dispersed back
into nothingness...

321
00:20:21,012 --> 00:20:23,014
we have a clean slate.

322
00:20:24,682 --> 00:20:27,143
It's like having all of
your sins wiped away.

323
00:20:28,269 --> 00:20:30,980
Sure you don't want
to write for the paper?

324
00:20:35,068 --> 00:20:37,737
What sins do you need
wiped away?

325
00:21:12,605 --> 00:21:14,565
GRACE: I used to be a vandal.

326
00:21:15,733 --> 00:21:16,776
HENRY: You did this?

327
00:21:17,986 --> 00:21:19,821
When I was 14.

328
00:21:21,864 --> 00:21:23,908
What is that,
like, Latin?

329
00:21:25,535 --> 00:21:27,870
What's it mean?
(INHALES DEEPLY)

330
00:21:30,373 --> 00:21:34,043
I'm tired.
I'm gonna... head home.

331
00:21:34,127 --> 00:21:35,378
(SNIFFS)
Uh...

332
00:21:38,006 --> 00:21:39,173
Do you want me to...

333
00:21:39,257 --> 00:21:41,801
Can you find your way back?

334
00:21:41,884 --> 00:21:44,554
Yeah. Uh...

335
00:21:44,637 --> 00:21:46,389
But are you all right? Yeah,
fine.

336
00:21:46,472 --> 00:21:49,100
Uh, thanks for hanging out.

337
00:21:49,892 --> 00:21:51,394
Um...

338
00:21:52,687 --> 00:21:55,148
Yeah, sure. Anytime.

339
00:21:56,607 --> 00:21:58,234
(PANTING SHARPLY)

340
00:21:58,317 --> 00:22:01,070
("HEART SKIPPED A BEAT"
BY THE XX PLAYING)

341
00:22:01,154 --> 00:22:02,238
(SIGHS)

342
00:22:07,452 --> 00:22:08,953
(VASE BREAKS)

343
00:22:14,751 --> 00:22:18,087
<i>♪ Please don't say we're done
When I'm not finished ♪</i>

344
00:22:18,171 --> 00:22:21,382
<i>♪ 'Cause I could give
So much more ♪</i>

345
00:22:22,633 --> 00:22:26,429
<i>♪ Make you feel
Like never before ♪</i>

346
00:22:26,512 --> 00:22:29,557
<i>♪ Welcome, they said
Welcome to the floor ♪</i>

347
00:22:29,640 --> 00:22:31,726
<i>♪ It's been a while
And you've found... ♪</i>

348
00:22:31,809 --> 00:22:33,144
Why are you still up?

349
00:22:33,227 --> 00:22:36,105
Um, just working on this.

350
00:22:39,025 --> 00:22:41,652
Is that the one Mom and
Dad got you on your birthday?

351
00:22:41,736 --> 00:22:43,863
Mm-hmm.

352
00:22:43,946 --> 00:22:46,407
Don't... hey.
It's still drying.

353
00:22:46,491 --> 00:22:47,909
Touchy, touchy, Henry.

354
00:22:47,992 --> 00:22:49,952
<i>♪ Sometimes I still Need you ♪</i>

355
00:22:50,036 --> 00:22:51,788
<i>♪ Sometimes I still need you ♪</i>

356
00:22:51,871 --> 00:22:53,331
<i>♪ Sometimes I still
Need you... ♪</i>

357
00:22:53,414 --> 00:22:57,710
It is an odd hobby
for a 17-year-old boy.

358
00:22:57,794 --> 00:23:00,630
<i>♪ Sometimes I still need you ♪</i>

359
00:23:09,013 --> 00:23:11,349
Did you know that, uh,
heartbreak triggers

360
00:23:11,432 --> 00:23:14,519
the same areas of the brain
as physical pain?

361
00:23:19,857 --> 00:23:22,485
When I think
about him leaving me,

362
00:23:22,568 --> 00:23:26,948
my brain sends a distress
signal to my nervous system.

363
00:23:27,031 --> 00:23:28,282
(SOBS QUIETLY)

364
00:23:28,366 --> 00:23:31,869
And it elevates my blood
pressure and my heart rate,

365
00:23:31,953 --> 00:23:33,663
giving me chest pains.

366
00:23:33,746 --> 00:23:36,833
(SOBS)
Okay.

367
00:23:36,916 --> 00:23:38,751
<i>♪ And I was struggling
To get in... ♪</i>

368
00:23:38,835 --> 00:23:39,961
(SOBS)

369
00:23:40,044 --> 00:23:41,754
It's happening right now.

370
00:23:41,838 --> 00:23:44,298
(SOBS) Do you want
me to go get Mom?

371
00:23:44,382 --> 00:23:45,675
Oh, God, no,
please.

372
00:23:45,758 --> 00:23:47,468
(SNIFFLING) Jesus.

373
00:23:47,552 --> 00:23:48,719
(GROANS)

374
00:23:53,391 --> 00:23:55,601
(SOBBING)

375
00:24:03,609 --> 00:24:06,612
(STUDENTS TALKING FAINTLY,
VOICES ECHOING)

376
00:24:08,197 --> 00:24:09,615
TEACHER: Henry.
Um...

377
00:24:09,699 --> 00:24:10,992
What's next?
Yeah.

378
00:24:11,075 --> 00:24:12,952
Um, uh, okay.

379
00:24:13,035 --> 00:24:16,539
Um, I was thinking we
all could kind of just put out

380
00:24:16,622 --> 00:24:18,791
a few ideas of themes
for the final issue.

381
00:24:18,875 --> 00:24:20,251
No time like the present.

382
00:24:20,334 --> 00:24:24,130
How about a trippy Alice
in Wonderland theme?

383
00:24:24,213 --> 00:24:26,132
You know, like,
we all dropped acid

384
00:24:26,215 --> 00:24:27,884
and went down the rabbit hole.

385
00:24:27,967 --> 00:24:30,219
Now,
that's tight. I could work with that.

386
00:24:30,303 --> 00:24:31,846
(EXHALES SHARPLY)

387
00:24:31,929 --> 00:24:32,930
Grace?

388
00:24:34,891 --> 00:24:35,892
What?

389
00:24:35,975 --> 00:24:37,476
TEACHER: Any ideas?

390
00:24:37,560 --> 00:24:38,811
I don't know.

391
00:24:38,895 --> 00:24:40,563
TEACHER: None?

392
00:24:40,646 --> 00:24:42,648
Yeah, that's what
"I don't know" means.

393
00:24:45,151 --> 00:24:46,903
Can I go to the bathroom?
I have to pee.

394
00:24:46,986 --> 00:24:48,988
Of course.

395
00:24:51,908 --> 00:24:53,576
LA: Just ask her what's going on.

396
00:24:53,659 --> 00:24:54,994
No, I don't know.

397
00:24:55,077 --> 00:24:57,622
I feel like I don't
know her like that yet.

398
00:24:57,705 --> 00:24:59,707
I don't know.
You should follow her.

399
00:25:01,042 --> 00:25:02,251
What?
Yeah.

400
00:25:02,335 --> 00:25:04,253
Don't you want to see
where she goes?

401
00:25:04,337 --> 00:25:06,839
(CHUCKLES) Oh. What if she's
hanging out with her boyfriend?

402
00:25:06,923 --> 00:25:09,050
(STIFLED LAUGH)

403
00:25:10,468 --> 00:25:11,719
(LAUGHS)
Oh, no.

404
00:25:11,802 --> 00:25:13,387
I'm not gonna stalk her.

405
00:25:21,812 --> 00:25:24,065
- (INSECTS TRILLING)
- (BIRDS CHIRPING)

406
00:26:30,631 --> 00:26:32,111
(QUIETLY) Maybe I
was looking for you.

407
00:26:32,174 --> 00:26:35,511
Clearly, I wasn't
going to see you.

408
00:26:35,594 --> 00:26:37,972
It just felt strange,

409
00:26:38,055 --> 00:26:41,350
wanting to tell you...
(ECHOES, FADES)

410
00:26:43,602 --> 00:26:45,229
(FOOTSTEPS THROUGH GRASS)

411
00:26:56,282 --> 00:26:57,533
(TYPING)

412
00:27:41,077 --> 00:27:43,829
- (PHONE CLICKS)
- (SIGHS)

413
00:27:43,913 --> 00:27:46,082
HENRY: Would you be down
to talk over maybe

414
00:27:46,165 --> 00:27:49,001
some theme ideas or something?
Whatever you pick is fine.

415
00:27:50,836 --> 00:27:51,962
Okay.

416
00:27:54,006 --> 00:27:57,635
Should we just, like... I just run
my final selections by you then, or...

417
00:27:57,718 --> 00:27:59,678
I mean, it really
doesn't matter to me.

418
00:27:59,762 --> 00:28:01,639
I just got a new
feature in from Jade,

419
00:28:01,722 --> 00:28:03,474
so I was just gonna edit that.

420
00:28:05,935 --> 00:28:07,269
(QUIETLY)
Oh.

421
00:28:08,062 --> 00:28:10,731
(BIRDS CHIRPING)

422
00:28:10,815 --> 00:28:11,857
(PHONE CHIMES)

423
00:28:18,906 --> 00:28:20,658
(SIGHS QUIETLY)

424
00:28:20,741 --> 00:28:22,159
(PHONE CLICKS)

425
00:28:22,243 --> 00:28:26,414
I'd like to match those,
but instead I will match this.

426
00:28:26,497 --> 00:28:28,124
Hey, Dad,
can I use the car?

427
00:28:28,207 --> 00:28:30,334
Uh, sure, sure.
Where to?

428
00:28:31,544 --> 00:28:33,379
Um... (LAUGHS)

429
00:28:33,462 --> 00:28:34,880
I don't know.
Nowhere, really.

430
00:28:34,964 --> 00:28:37,216
So, where are you going?

431
00:28:37,299 --> 00:28:39,510
It's not a cross-examination.
(LAUGHS)

432
00:28:39,593 --> 00:28:41,720
I mean,
it feels like that.

433
00:28:41,804 --> 00:28:44,140
Well, you're asking us
to lend you our car,

434
00:28:44,223 --> 00:28:46,142
and we're asking a
simple question in return.

435
00:28:46,225 --> 00:28:49,645
Okay, um, I'm just... I don't
know. I'm just gonna go to Muz's.

436
00:28:49,728 --> 00:28:51,939
Now can I have the keys?
You're not even using it.

437
00:28:52,940 --> 00:28:54,525
You're entitled to your privacy.

438
00:28:54,608 --> 00:28:57,236
If you don't want us to know something,
say so.

439
00:28:57,319 --> 00:28:59,196
But don't insult us with a lie.

440
00:28:59,280 --> 00:29:01,657
It's beneath you.

441
00:29:01,740 --> 00:29:03,325
Just... You can Uber.

442
00:29:03,409 --> 00:29:05,411
What? I can't Uber. I
don't have a credit card.

443
00:29:05,494 --> 00:29:07,955
Well, then you're
gonna have to walk.

444
00:29:08,038 --> 00:29:10,541
(SIGHING)

445
00:29:18,215 --> 00:29:20,509
(LIGHTS BUZZING)

446
00:29:23,512 --> 00:29:25,764
(GRACE PANTING SHARPLY)

447
00:29:30,019 --> 00:29:31,729
(GRUNTS)

448
00:29:31,812 --> 00:29:33,939
(PANTING)

449
00:29:44,617 --> 00:29:45,951
(GRUNTS)

450
00:29:51,790 --> 00:29:53,584
(GRUNTS)

451
00:29:53,667 --> 00:29:56,003
Fuck! (SHOUTS)

452
00:30:01,926 --> 00:30:04,136
(SOBBING QUIETLY)

453
00:30:08,474 --> 00:30:09,892
(PANTING)

454
00:30:09,975 --> 00:30:13,062
(SOBBING ECHOES)

455
00:30:13,145 --> 00:30:15,272
(GROANING)

456
00:30:20,194 --> 00:30:21,445
(PHONE CLICKS)

457
00:30:21,529 --> 00:30:23,489
(TYPING)

458
00:30:40,381 --> 00:30:42,675
(TYPING)

459
00:30:45,678 --> 00:30:46,679
(PHONE CLICKS)

460
00:30:46,762 --> 00:30:48,264
(SIGHS)

461
00:30:50,683 --> 00:30:52,768
(MURMURING)

462
00:30:55,980 --> 00:30:57,690
Hi. Hey.
What's up?

463
00:30:57,773 --> 00:30:59,525
So, I couldn't sleep last night,

464
00:30:59,608 --> 00:31:01,735
so I was searching
through Mrs. Klein's syllabus,

465
00:31:01,819 --> 00:31:06,198
and I noticed a very
interesting trend.

466
00:31:09,118 --> 00:31:10,619
Uh...

467
00:31:13,247 --> 00:31:14,373
Look at them.
Okay.

468
00:31:16,041 --> 00:31:18,919
HENRY: Um, yeah.

469
00:31:19,003 --> 00:31:21,589
They're, um,
all about young people?

470
00:31:21,672 --> 00:31:24,049
GRACE: And they
all involve suicide.

471
00:31:24,133 --> 00:31:26,468
Romeo and Juliet off themselves.

472
00:31:26,552 --> 00:31:27,761
Werther shoots himself.

473
00:31:27,845 --> 00:31:29,597
Holden contemplates suicide.

474
00:31:29,680 --> 00:31:32,516
Conrad tries to kill himself
by slashing his wrists.

475
00:31:34,226 --> 00:31:36,520
This is what you were up
thinking about last night?

476
00:31:36,604 --> 00:31:37,896
Yeah.

477
00:31:39,690 --> 00:31:41,567
Is that normal?
Oh, Jesus.

478
00:31:41,650 --> 00:31:43,861
What?
Well, I... (SIGHS)

479
00:31:43,944 --> 00:31:45,237
You...

480
00:31:46,447 --> 00:31:49,950
You, uh, like,
seemed down this week,

481
00:31:50,034 --> 00:31:52,661
so that's a little scary,
I guess. Okay.

482
00:31:54,788 --> 00:31:56,373
How do I explain this?

483
00:31:56,457 --> 00:31:58,917
Um... okay.

484
00:31:59,001 --> 00:32:02,838
Think about what it means
to be a teenager. Okay?

485
00:32:02,921 --> 00:32:05,716
Your parents
pressure you to succeed.

486
00:32:05,799 --> 00:32:09,678
Your friends pressure you to
do shit you don't want to do.

487
00:32:09,762 --> 00:32:11,805
Social media pressures
you to hate your body.

488
00:32:11,889 --> 00:32:16,352
It's hard, even if you're a
well-adjusted kid from a good family.

489
00:32:16,435 --> 00:32:17,686
Okay.

490
00:32:17,770 --> 00:32:20,773
So, now imagine you can't be
with the person that you love,

491
00:32:20,856 --> 00:32:23,150
like Romeo and Juliet
or Werther,

492
00:32:23,233 --> 00:32:25,152
or you lose the person
that you love,

493
00:32:25,235 --> 00:32:27,154
like Holden or Conrad.

494
00:32:27,237 --> 00:32:29,990
There's a reason why,
when every author,

495
00:32:30,074 --> 00:32:33,202
from Shakespeare to Salinger,
writes about young people,

496
00:32:33,285 --> 00:32:37,039
they can't avoid
the truth, that...

497
00:32:37,122 --> 00:32:40,334
being young is so painful,

498
00:32:40,417 --> 00:32:44,630
it's almost, like,
too much to feel.

499
00:32:45,839 --> 00:32:47,716
Yeah, are you, like...

500
00:32:49,468 --> 00:32:50,678
suicidal or...

501
00:32:50,761 --> 00:32:52,846
No. God.
I mean...

502
00:32:53,972 --> 00:32:55,891
Look, I'm not gonna kill myself.

503
00:32:55,974 --> 00:33:01,271
But have I thought about what it would
be like to just not be here anymore?

504
00:33:01,355 --> 00:33:02,606
Yeah.

505
00:33:02,690 --> 00:33:06,318
And I don't say that casually,
but I do say it

506
00:33:06,402 --> 00:33:11,407
